<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Tudor is a 69-year-old woman treated for major depression, one week after starting sertraline 25 mg, who came in the same day with a sore throat for two days. It began with a runny nose and sneezing after several of her staff had colds, and she now has a dry cough, a hoarse voice, and pain on swallowing. Her temperature at home was 37.8 C last night. She can swallow liquids and soft food, though she has eaten even less than usual, and she is drinking about a liter a day. She has no trouble opening her mouth, no drooling, no difficulty breathing, no ear pain, no rash, and no neck stiffness. She has taken no throat lozenges or cold remedies, remembering the advice about over-the-counter night-time products. She has had no nausea, diarrhea, headache, restlessness, or bleeding on sertraline. Her mood is unchanged; she has had no thoughts of death or of harming herself since the first visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days; runny nose first, then sore throat and dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Pain on swallowing; liquids and soft food manageable</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Low-grade fever, hoarseness, dry cough, runny nose</td></tr><tr><td>Pertinent negatives</td><td>No trismus, drooling, breathing difficulty, ear pain, rash, or neck stiffness</td></tr><tr><td>Exposure</td><td>Colds among her household staff</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Sertraline 25 mg for one week, well tolerated; no cold remedies</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

